A||||17||Eric Marsh||New V8 Fiero Web Site Up||||1
Z||000000||Eric Marsh||10-21-1999||12:22 PM||||The nice folks at hotrodder.com have given me the web page space for my on going Fiero project. Its at <A HREF="http://www.hotrodder.com/emarsh" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hotrodder.com/emarsh</A>.  Eventually this thing will see the light of day.<P>Eric<P>[This message has been edited by Eric Marsh (edited 10-21-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by Eric Marsh (edited 10-21-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||10-22-1999||12:08 AM||||Dang Eric! I'm super impressed and a bit jealous. The attention to detail and the amount of time and $$$ spent on that engine is awesome.<P>I have a chance to buy a new set of Trick Flow heads right now. A guy bought them for his Vette and then decided to build a big block instead. After seeing your site, I'll probably buy them now.<P>I'm in the process of building a 1988 Z-28, roller cam, 4 bolt main, 355 engine for my Batmobile Fiero. You'll still have a few cubes and a couple extra ponies more then me, so I'll be careful not to race any white 88 Fieros in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||10-22-1999||12:59 AM||||Eric, I noticed you autocrossed your Fiero. Did you have a V8 in it at that time? How did you do?<P>Last Sunday I autocrossed my 88 Coupe V6 and got better lap times than a 94 Turbo Surpra, 90 300ZX TT, several Camaros and a 99 C5 Vette!!! I don't think he was happy when I asked him what his best lap was.<P> You went only the one time? You didn't get hooked like I did? <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000003||DJRice||10-22-1999||08:50 AM||||Wow!  That car will be bad a__ when it gets finished.  It probably will need tires almost as often as it needs gas.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric Marsh||10-22-1999||12:42 PM||||To: SCCA Fiero<P>Hi,<P>Regarding autocrossing, the V8 has not hit the street yet, so I was driving the stock V6. I didn't do as well as I would have liked to mostly because of a bad front-end washout problem. I took my wife on the first ride I took around the track, and 7 seconds in I was  driving sideways and a little backwards around the cones. She said something about here I am spinning the car already and I said no way, and drove it back out while only taking out a cone or two. :-)<P>I've done a bit of racing in the past including motorcycle roadracing and running a Pro-Stock motorcycle. My solution to the problem of a car that won't run at a competition could be to drive less agressively, or to try to bring the car up to a level where I can drive it really hard. I chose the second option. I'm guessing that the folks at the autocross track will be a bit surprised when I take it back to be tech'ed next time. :-)<p>[This message has been edited by Eric Marsh (edited 10-22-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||10-24-1999||09:36 PM||||That is one nice FFFFFFiero, But tell us Eric how do you change the fire wall side plugs????||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-24-1999||09:44 PM||||That has got to be the most header pipe I have ever seen! I just cant help but wonder- how does a 406 sound with 180 headers? I have heard a small block with a 180 crankshaft- even though the team owners denied it at first- and it had a most unusual sound to it, more like an Indy car.||||reg||5
Z||000007||Phil||10-25-1999||05:26 AM||||Eric<BR>How do you plan to handle your front end wash out problem. I may be all wet but wouldn't more weight and HP in the rear aggravate the situation?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric Marsh||10-25-1999||11:17 AM||||More answers:<P>DXR_DAD:<P>Its pretty easy. To change the plugs on the back you just go up from the bottom. Number 7 looks like its going to be a trick though.<P>Cooter:<P>I've run the motor but not on the street. I've head 180 degree headers before myself and they sounded like a big 6 cyl, but this set doesn't seem to have that sound. Might be because about 12" out of the collector I merge both collectors together into a single 3 1/2" pipe. Course, I have not run it that much and it might sound different on the street. But as is, I'm really happy with the SpinTech muffler, which is supposed to be super low restriction yet its as quiet as a 3 chamber FlowMaster. The car has a "big" sound but its not real noisy. You don't hear the individual pulses like you would with glass packs. Sounds more like a big block with duel low restriction turbo mufflers or somthing like that. I'm thinking of doing a recording of it and putting it on my web page.<P>Phil:<P>For starters, to fix the understeer problem I'm going to put as much sticky tire up front as I can. I'm also rebuilding the front suspension and making sure that everythings right. From there, if it still doesn't work I'm going to go back to the drawing board and study the current suspension's engineering.<P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||10-25-1999||09:30 PM||||This is my plan to get my V8 Fiero to handle better: 1) rebuild the entire suspension using polyurethane bushings, 2) rebuild the steering system, 3) remove weight from the rear, such as, moving the battery to the front and changing to aluminum heads, 4) adding a rear sway bar.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Fiero||10-25-1999||10:21 PM||||Anyone thought of changing the spring rate in the front to counter balance the rear????||||reg||1
Z||000011||cmyokley||10-26-1999||12:03 PM||||I sure wish I could SEE what you all are talking about here. I have tried many times to click on the link to your site and it will not upload. Dang. Could you e-mail me a pic.<P>Thanks||||reg||8
Z||000012||cmyokley||10-26-1999||02:08 PM||||never mind.....I was not giving enough time to load the site....<P>Great looking headers and motor. Must be nice to have a pipe bender in your back pocket!!!||||reg||14
Z||000013||Eric Marsh||10-26-1999||05:28 PM||||I am mounting the battery up front now. Still trying to figure out just how to to it.<P>As for having a pipe bender in my back pocket, those headers were actually made out of lots of mandrel bends. Its my first time out doing that sort of work, and hopefully the next job will be a little cleaner.<P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||10-26-1999||05:48 PM||||Eric, an automotive battery will fit under the spare tire up in the front compartment. I bought a special Fiero kit, but I've heard that lots of people fabricate something themselves or just mount a battery box from JEGS or Summit under there.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Eric Marsh||10-26-1999||06:06 PM||||I've got a stainless steel battery tray from Summit racing and  I took a saw to the spare tire well last night. Looks like battery is going to have to sit on top of the steering rack. That means some sort of a mounting system.<P>I'm sure its perfectly do-able, but right now I'm looking at it, rubbing my jaw, and saying "hmmmm......"||||reg||1
Z||000016||tyruss||10-28-1999||01:10 PM||||Just a few questions. What transmission are you planning to use? 4-Speed, 5-Speed or Automatic? (probably not an auto, but it doesn't hurt to ask). What manufacturer? (Standard, Gertag, Muncie, Isuzu, Porsche or some other).  Who's adapter kit are you using? (V8 Archie, Zumalt or your own creation).  Are you concerned about the axels being different lengths? (wouldn't this cause the car to "take-off" one way or the other?).<BR>  Are you planning any cooling modifications? (4 core radiator and/or cutting a hole in the hood or spare tire compartment for better air flow).  The reason for the 20 questions is, I have a 327, bored .60 over in the works and I am looking at the issues that are still down the road. Any info would appreciated. Thanks Freddy.||||reg||12
Z||000017||Eric Marsh||10-28-1999||02:15 PM||||Hi Freddie,<P>Answering your questions:<P>I'm using a THM-125 automatic modified by Jim's performance Transmissions.<P>The kit is by V8Archie.<P>I'm using Archie's 4 core radiator.<P>I really don't see why the different axle lengths should be an issue.<P>Eric||||reg||1
